### JWT Auth flow — spring-security-jwt-authorization
# Run these IN ORDER:
#   1. Register a user
#   2. Login -> the access_token is captured automatically into {{token}}
#   3. Call the protected endpoints using that token
#
# The login URL is /authorization (configured by jwt.url in application.yml),
# handled by JwtAuthenticationFilter, NOT a @Controller method.

@host = http://localhost:8080

### 1. Register a normal USER (public endpoint)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/public/accounts/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "alice",
  "password": "password123",
  "email": "alice@example.com",
  "full_name": "Alice Example",
  "roles": ["USER"]
}

### 1b. Register an ADMIN (to test the admin-only endpoint)
POST {{host}}/api/v1/public/accounts/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "boss",
  "password": "password123",
  "email": "boss@example.com",
  "full_name": "The Boss",
  "roles": ["ADMIN"]
}

### 2. Login -> capture the access_token into the {{token}} variable
# After this runs, {{token}} is reused by the requests below.
POST {{host}}/authorization
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "alice",
  "password": "password123"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("token", response.body.access_token);
    client.log("Captured access_token: " + response.body.access_token);
%}

### 3. Call a USER endpoint (needs USER or ADMIN authority)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/user/accounts
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 4. Call the ADMIN endpoint with alice's token -> expect 403 Forbidden
# (alice is only a USER). Log in as "boss" above to get through.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/admin/accounts
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 5. Call a protected endpoint with NO token -> expect 401 Unauthorized
GET {{host}}/api/v1/user/accounts
